     A BEAT IS A CHORD TURNED ON ITS SIDE

this page is drawn on its side. tilt your head left for the axis,
right for the tracklist. the titles hang from the top the way the
drums do in this music: what was high comes first.


    scripts/play.sh 600


THE RECORD, ONE CHARACTER PER FOUR BARS

each strip is the angle of the picture, phrase by phrase (7.68 s).

    ─  0°    as drawn: a chord          ╱  turning: glissandi bending
    │  90°   turned: a groove           ╲  turning past 90° or 270°
    ━  180°  backwards, negative        ┃  270°: the drum kit swapped
             harmony                    ┼  both readings at once
    ~ ≈      small mirror bends, left ear one way, right ear the other
    ╎  sheared (swing)   ⋰  lasers   ╻  tape stop   ║  half time   ¦  double

 1  clock_flip       0:46   ─│─│┼┼
       a chord, then the same picture through transpose=clock_flip

 2  quarter-turn     3:04   ──╱╱╱╱││││││││─╱││││││╱╱
       house; the riser is the rotation, the drop is ninety degrees

 3  pinpoint         2:49   ──││││││││╱╱││││││││──
       a sentence in braille is the drum machine; flat dots ghost

 4  sieve of life    2:49   ──││││││──╱╱││││││││──
       conway's life through a xenakis sieve: a melody, turned a groove

 5  half-turn        0:38   ━━━━━
       interlude: a chorale played backwards in negative harmony

 6  symplectic       3:04   ──││││╎╎╎╎⋰⋰╻║║║║¦╎╎╎╎──
       the groove sheared and squeezed: swing, lasers, tape stop

 7  revolution       3:50   ──────╱╱││││││╲╲━━━━╱╱┃┃┃┃┃┃╲╲
       two perpendicular readings make one full turn

 8  prolation        3:04   ────~~~~────≈≈≈≈────╱╱╱│
       a canon at three speeds that ends by turning into its own clock


HOW IT WORKS, IN ONE PARAGRAPH

spectrumsynth plays a picture: columns are moments, rows are
frequencies. at 12800 samples per second, fft 1024, hop 256, six
pixels are both a sixteenth note at 125 bpm and 75 Hz, so harmonic p
of 75 Hz is the same row as sixteenth-step p. draw a chord, turn the
picture a quarter, and the same pixels are a beat. four-on-the-floor
is a sawtooth. offbeat hats are a square wave. a kick is a harmonic
that only speaks at the start of the phrase. the riser is the picture
tilting. the drop is the moment it stands upright.


